  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 135 AM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nThe beginning of the Long Term forecast remains on track for \nincreased shower/storm chances and cooler temperatures thanks to an \nanticipated cold front. Increased lift and moisture ahead of the \ncold front bring low to medium (10-40%) shower/storm chances across \nsoutheast New Mexico, the northwest Permian Basin, and Upper/Lower \nTrans-Pecos Saturday night. At this time, the best chances of \nconvection look to be over southeast New Mexico. Lower heights \nbehind the cold front yield highs in the 90s for most on Sunday, \nwith the hottest locations along the Rio Grande where readings are \nexpected to reach or exceed the century mark. \n\nThe upper-level ridge begins to build into our region by early next \nweek, allowing for afternoon highs to climb back into the lower \ntriple digits for many. These locations include much of the Permian \nBasin, southeast New Mexico, and the Trans Pecos. The hottest areas \ncontinue to be for those along the Rio Grande, especially in low-\nlying spots of the Big Bend. By mid-to-late next week, long-range \nmodel guidance depict the upper-level ridge breaking apart as a \ntrough digs into the Pacific Northwest. Lower heights associated \nwith this pattern suggests that another slight cool-down will be in \nstore, with highs mainly in the upper 90s. Overnight lows continue \nto be expected across most locations each night, with 60s in/near \nthe higher terrain. \n  \nGreening",
